    "pthm-emg-local-electrovacuum-model": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "post-emg-einstein-maxwell-action",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Use the smooth oriented, time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold, curvature\nconvention and SI constants of [[def-emg-geometry-units]] and\n[[def-emg-lorentzian-forms]]. Let $F$ be the curvature two-form of a smooth\nconnection in one fixed bundle sector. Choose the actual matter functional\n$S_m$ of [[post-emg-selected-matter-action]]. For configurations where the\naction is finite, or for compactly supported action differences on a\nnoncompact manifold, adopt\n\n$$S_{\\mathrm{tot}}[g,A,\\Psi] =\\frac{c^3}{16\\pi G_N}\\int_M(R-2\\Lambda)\\,\\mathrm{vol}_g -\\frac1{4\\mu_0c}\\int_M F_{ab}F^{ab}\\,\\mathrm{vol}_g +S_m[g,A,\\Psi].$$\n\nAdopt that a configuration is dynamically admissible in this branch exactly\nwhen the first variation of this total action vanishes independently for\nevery smooth compactly supported symmetric inverse-metric variation, every\nsmooth compactly supported fixed-sector connection one-form variation, and\nevery admissible compactly supported matter-field variation. If $M$ has a\nphysical boundary, only variations vanishing on a neighbourhood of that\nboundary are included. This makes no claim about a Gibbons–Hawking–York or\nother boundary action.\n\nThe earlier postulate [[post-em-stationary-action-model]] concerns its stated\nflat electromagnetic and prescribed-potential particle sectors. It supplies\nno unified matter–gravity action or coupled-solution theorem; the functional\nabove is an additional, explicitly adopted model. An equation-based\nEinstein–Maxwell formulation is a separate alternative, not a premise of this\naction branch.\n\nAll displayed coefficients are model parameters with the units and\ninterpretations in [[def-emg-geometry-units]]. Compact support is essential:\nthe Einstein–Hilbert and Maxwell integrations by parts have boundary terms in\ngeneral. The postulate asserts neither that its stationary configurations exist\nnor that the action describes nature.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "pthm-emg-action-field-equations",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g)$, the fixed smooth connection sector, the constants $c,G_N,\\mu_0>0$,\nconstant $\\Lambda$, and the selected matter functional $S_m$ be exactly those\nof [[post-emg-einstein-maxwell-action]]. Write $F$ for the connection curvature,\n$j$ and $T^m$ for the current and Hilbert tensor in the selected matter\nvariation, and\n\n$$T^{\\mathrm{EM}}_{ab}:=\\frac1{\\mu_0}\\left(F_{ac}F_b{}^c-\\frac14g_{ab}F_{cd}F^{cd}\\right),\\qquad \\kappa_E:=\\frac{8\\pi G_N}{c^4}.$$\n\nThe homogeneous identity $dF=0$ holds because $F$ is the curvature of the\nsmooth connection in the fixed sector. The adopted configuration is stationary\nunder every admissible compactly supported metric, connection, and matter\nvariation if and only if all of the following hold:\n\n$$\\nabla_aF^{ab}=-\\mu_0j^b,\\qquad G_{ab}+\\Lambda g_{ab}=\\kappa_E(T^{\\mathrm{EM}}_{ab}+T^m_{ab}),\\qquad \\delta_\\Psi S_m[\\zeta]=0\\ \\text{for every admissible compactly supported }\\zeta.$$\n\nThe last clause is the selected matter model's Euler–Lagrange condition; an\nexplicit local matter equation cannot be given until $S_m$ is specified.\nThese are model deductions from the adopted action and smooth compact\nvariation hypotheses, not evidence that a coupled solution exists or that the\nmodel describes nature.",
          "uses": [
            "3.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-emg-geometry-units",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Adopt SI units and the following model-local quantity roles. Here the selected Einstein–Maxwell framework combines the stated Lorentzian geometry with a classical electromagnetic two-form and current; this definition itself supplies no gravitational equation, matter action, or empirical claim. A primitive is an input or independent field variable of this formulation; a derived quantity is fixed by a stated construction from its inputs. Neither label is universal across physical theories, and unit choice alone does not determine it.\n\nThe spacetime data are a smooth, Hausdorff, second-countable, boundaryless four-manifold $M$, a smooth Lorentzian metric $g$ of signature $(-,+,+,+)$, a smooth time-orientation field, and an independent orientation, exactly as in [[def-emg-lorentzian-forms]]. The metric, its time orientation, and the manifold orientation are separate data. An observer at $p\\in M$ is a future unit timelike vector $n\\in T_pM$ with $g(n,n)=-1$. A smooth observer congruence is a smooth field of such vectors. A local tetrad is a smooth basis $(e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ with metric matrix $\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$; an observer congruence, a coordinate chart, and a tetrad are different objects. Tetrads and observer fields need only exist locally; this definition assumes no global inertial chart, global tetrad, or hypersurface-orthogonal congruence.\n\nThe electromagnetic field-strength variable $F$ is a smooth real two-form, hence an alternating covariant tensor of type $(0,2)$, on an open subset of $M$, as in [[def-emg-lorentzian-forms]]. It is primitive in this formulation. For an observer congruence $n$, its electric and magnetic observer covectors are derived by\n\n$$E_a:=cF_{ab}n^b,\\qquad B_a:=-({\\star_gF})_{ab}n^b.$$\n\nHere $\\star_g$ is the unique Hodge map characterized pointwise by $\\alpha\\wedge\\star_g\\beta=\\langle\\alpha,\\beta\\rangle_g\\,\\mathrm{vol}_g$, using the metric and independent orientation, as in [[lem-emg-lorentz-hodge-calculus]]. Both covectors annihilate $n$ and lie in the rest covector space $\\{\\alpha\\in T_p^*M:\\alpha(n)=0\\}$, whose induced metric is positive definite. In a positively oriented future orthonormal tetrad with $e_0=n$ and $\\epsilon_{123}=+1$, their spatial components obey the fixed dictionary $F_{0i}=-E_i/c$ and $F_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$ from [[lem-emg-observer-field-decomposition]]. Thus $E$ and $B$ are observer-dependent derived fields, not globally defined observer-independent three-vectors on a curved spacetime. Their tetrad components have units $\\mathrm{V\\,m^{-1}}$ and tesla (T), respectively; the components of $F$ in a length-coordinate chart have unit T. No global potential is assumed; when a local potential $A_a$ is used, its components have unit $\\mathrm{T\\,m}$ and a scalar potential $\\varphi$ has unit V.\n\nFor any chosen observer $n$, a smooth four-current is a vector field $j$ whose local decomposition is $j=c\\rho\\,n+J$, where $\\rho:=-g(n,j)/c$ is the observer charge density and $J:=j-c\\rho n$ is orthogonal to $n$. In the associated positively oriented orthonormal tetrad its components are $j^0=c\\rho$ and $j^i=J^i$. The units are $[\\rho]=\\mathrm{C\\,m^{-3}}$, $[J^i]=[j^a]=\\mathrm{A\\,m^{-2}}$, and $[q]=\\mathrm C$ for a charge parameter supplied by a matter model. The current is a prescribed input or is supplied by an explicitly stated matter model; it is not inferred from the Maxwell equation alone.\n\nThe SI constants and coordinate units are fixed as follows. The speed of ideal vacuum light $c>0$ is the exact SI defining constant $299\\,792\\,458\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$; identifying it with the shared ideal-light and clock conversion constant is the adopted Minkowski model of [[post-em-minkowski-physical-model]], not a consequence of its unit definition. The elementary charge $e$ is exactly $1.602\\,176\\,634\\times10^{-19}\\ \\mathrm C$. These unit definitions do not imply that charge is quantized. The magnetic constant $\\mu_0>0$ has unit $\\mathrm{N\\,A^{-2}}$ and is experimentally determined in the revised SI, so no exact numerical value for $\\mu_0$ is adopted. Define the electric constant (vacuum permittivity) $\\varepsilon_0:=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ in $\\mathrm{F\\,m^{-1}}$; hence $\\varepsilon_0\\mu_0c^2=1$, and $\\varepsilon_0$ has the same relative standard uncertainty as $\\mu_0$ because $c$ is exact. The exact component equation $\\nabla_aF^{ab}=-\\mu_0j^b$ is dimensionally consistent: both sides have unit $\\mathrm{T\\,m^{-1}}$ in a length-coordinate frame.\n\nIn a length-coordinate chart all four coordinate values are assigned metres, with $x^0=ct$ when a time coordinate $t$ in seconds is selected. This is local coordinate bookkeeping, not a global inertial-coordinate claim. In such a chart $g_{ab}$ is dimensionless, the Levi-Civita coefficients $\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}$ have unit $\\mathrm{m^{-1}}$, and curvature components have unit $\\mathrm{m^{-2}}$, as in [[def-emg-lorentzian-forms]]. Components in other coordinate units transform by their Jacobians.\n\nFor the gravitational sector, take Newton's gravitational constant $G_N>0$ with unit $\\mathrm{m^3\\,kg^{-1}\\,s^{-2}}$ and the cosmological constant parameter $\\Lambda\\in\\mathbb R$ with unit $\\mathrm{m^{-2}}$ as primitive parameters of the selected model; no numerical value for either is used here. The coupling normalization $\\kappa_E:=8\\pi G_N/c^4$ is derived from these parameters and has unit $\\mathrm{m\\,J^{-1}}$. This definition records the convention for a later Einstein equation; it asserts no Einstein equation. A separate positive normalization $\\kappa_0$ with unit weber (Wb) is a chosen flux conversion reserved for a later explicitly specified $U(1)$ formulation, where $U(1):=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C:|z|=1\\}$ under multiplication. It is not $\\kappa_E$, an electric charge, $\\mu_0$, or Planck's constant, and it implies no charge quantization. Test-particle rest mass $m>0$ in kg and charge $q$ in C are externally supplied matter parameters, not quantities derived by classical electromagnetism.\n\nThese are SI and model-definition conventions only. They neither assert empirical measurements beyond the stated SI defining constants and measured status of $\\mu_0$, nor identify similarly named quantities in another framework.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-emg-electrovacuum-cauchy-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,N,o)$ be a smooth, Hausdorff, second-countable, oriented and time-oriented four-manifold with smooth Lorentz metric of signature $(-,+,+,+)$, as in [[def-emg-lorentzian-forms]]. Use its metric-selected connection and curvature convention. Fix $c>0$, $G_N>0$, $\\mu_0>0$, and $\\Lambda\\in\\mathbb R$, and put\n\n$$\\kappa:=\\frac{8\\pi G_N}{c^4}>0.$$\n\nThese are parameters of the mathematical system. For the SI interpretation, $c$ has units $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, $G_N$ has units $\\mathrm{m^3\\,kg^{-1}\\,s^{-2}}$, $\\mu_0$ has units $\\mathrm{N\\,A^{-2}}$, $\\kappa$ has units $\\mathrm{s^2\\,kg^{-1}\\,m^{-1}}$, and $\\Lambda$ has units $\\mathrm{m^{-2}}$. In a length-coordinate chart $x^0=ct$, metric components are dimensionless and curvature components have units $\\mathrm{m^{-2}}$.\n\nFor a smooth real two-form $F\\in\\Omega^2(M)$, define\n\n$$Q_{ab}(g,F):=F_{ac}F_b{}^c-\\frac14 g_{ab}F_{cd}F^{cd},\\qquad T^{\\rm EM}_{ab}:=\\mu_0^{-1}Q_{ab}(g,F).$$\n\nIndices are raised with $g^{-1}$; $\\star_g$ is the Lorentzian Hodge star of [[lem-emg-lorentz-hodge-calculus]]. The electromagnetic stress tensor $T^{\\rm EM}$ is a symmetric covariant two-tensor; in an orthonormal frame its components have energy-density units $\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}$. If $n$ is a future unit normal and $(\\mathfrak e_1,\\mathfrak e_2,\\mathfrak e_3)$ is a positively oriented orthonormal spatial triad, define $e_i:=F(\\mathfrak e_i,n)$ and $b_i:=\\tfrac12\\epsilon_i{}^{jk}F(\\mathfrak e_j,\\mathfrak e_k)$. By [[lem-emg-observer-field-decomposition]], these are $E_i/c$ and $B_i$ in the SI observer convention; both have units tesla.\n\nFor sign clarity only, a sourced extension would write $\\nabla_aF^{ab}=-\\mu_0J^b$ for a smooth current vector $J$; the source-free system below is the case $J=0$.\n\nThe **source-free electrovacuum tensor Cauchy system** on an open set $U\\subseteq M$ is the system for a smooth Lorentz metric $g$ and smooth real two-form $F$ on $U$ given by\n\n$$G_{ab}[g]+\\Lambda g_{ab}=\\kappa T^{\\rm EM}_{ab}[g,F],\\qquad dF=0,\\qquad d(\\star_gF)=0.$$\n\nHere $G_{ab}=\\operatorname{Ric}_{ab}-\\tfrac12 Sg_{ab}$ is the Einstein tensor. A **spacelike initial hypersurface** is a smooth spacelike embedded three-manifold $i:\\Sigma\\hookrightarrow U$ with induced Riemannian metric $h=i^*g$, future unit normal $n$, and second fundamental form $K(X,Y)=g(\\nabla_Xn,Y)=\\tfrac12(\\mathcal L_nh)(X,Y)$, using the convention of [[def-gr-spacelike-initial-data-and-constraints]]. It is a **Cauchy hypersurface in $U$** exactly when every inextendible causal curve $\\gamma:I\\to U$ meets it at exactly one parameter value, that is, $\\gamma^{-1}[i(\\Sigma)]$ has exactly one element, using the causal-curve convention of [[def-gr-spacelike-hypersurfaces-and-cauchy-sets]]. The local initial-data constructions require a spacelike initial hypersurface; they do not assume this global intersection property. The initial data are the induced $(h,K,e,b)$, where $e,b$ are the spatial fields just defined. No global electromagnetic potential is part of this definition.\n\nIn the SI convention the electromagnetic two-form has orthonormal-frame components in tesla, $T^{\\rm EM}_{ab}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}$, and every term of the Einstein equation has units $\\mathrm{m^{-2}}$. The equations define a mathematical model; they do not assert that the model describes nature or that arbitrary Cauchy data have a development.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-emg-constrained-electrovacuum-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\Sigma$ be a nonempty, smooth, oriented three-manifold without boundary. Let $h$ be a smooth Riemannian metric on $\\Sigma$, let $K$ be a smooth symmetric covariant two-tensor, and let $e,b$ be smooth vector fields tangent to $\\Sigma$. Let $D$ and $R(h)$ be the Levi-Civita connection and scalar curvature of $h$, set $\\tau:=\\operatorname{tr}_hK$ and $|K|_h^2:=h^{ik}h^{j\\ell}K_{ij}K_{k\\ell}$, and choose the induced spatial orientation so $\\operatorname{vol}_g=\\theta^0\\wedge\\operatorname{vol}_h=-n^\\flat\\wedge\\operatorname{vol}_h$ in a positively oriented normal coframe with $\\theta^0(n)=1$. Define the spatial cross product by $h(e\\times_hb,w)=\\operatorname{vol}_h(e,b,w)$ for each tangent vector $w$.\n\nFor the fixed parameters $(\\kappa,\\Lambda,\\mu_0)$ of [[def-emg-electrovacuum-cauchy-system]], the tuple $(\\Sigma,h,K,e,b)$ is **constrained electrovacuum initial data** when\n\n$$D_ie^i=0,\\qquad D_ib^i=0,$$\n\n$$R(h)+\\tau^2-|K|_h^2=2\\Lambda+\\frac{\\kappa}{\\mu_0}\\bigl(|e|_h^2+|b|_h^2\\bigr),$$\n\n$$D^jK_{ji}-D_i\\tau=-\\frac{\\kappa}{\\mu_0}(e\\times_hb)_i.$$\n\nHere the last display is an equality of covectors. The scalar-curvature form is the Gauss-Codazzi form of the normal projections in [[thm-gr-constraint-equations-in-normal-frame]]; the sign and $K$ convention are those of [[def-gr-spacelike-initial-data-and-constraints]] and [[def-emg-electrovacuum-cauchy-system]]. This definition asserts no existence or uniqueness of a spacetime development.\n\nFor the periodic high-regularity class, take $\\Sigma=\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi}$, integer $s\\ge5$, $h\\in H^{s+1}$, $K,e,b\\in H^s$, and assume the continuous representative of $h$ has a uniform positive lower eigenvalue bound. The displayed constraint expressions then have continuous representatives: their weak-derivative orders are at most two for $h$ and one for $K,e,b$, and the embedding, algebra and smooth-composition suppliers [[lem-gr-periodic-sobolev-embedding]], [[lem-gr-periodic-sobolev-algebra]], and [[lem-gr-smooth-composition-and-commutator-estimates]] apply. In SI coordinates $K$ and $D$ carry one inverse-length factor, $R(h)$ and $\\Lambda$ carry inverse-length squared, while $e,b$ have units tesla; the coefficients $\\kappa/\\mu_0$ give the displayed terms matching units.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-emg-local-high-sobolev-electrovacuum",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $s\\ge5$ be an integer, $\\kappa>0$, $\\mu_0>0$, and $\\Lambda\\in\\mathbb R$. On $\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi}=\\mathbb R^3/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)^3$, let $h\\in H^{s+1}$ be a symmetric Riemannian metric with $h\\ge c_0 I$ for some $c_0>0$, let $K\\in H^s$ be a symmetric covariant two-tensor, and let $e,b\\in H^s$ be spatial vector fields. Here $H^s$ is the real componentwise Fourier Sobolev space of [[def-emg-periodic-high-sobolev-state]]. Use the length coordinate $t=x^0=ct_{\\rm SI}$, so $g,h$ are dimensionless, $K$ has units $\\mathrm m^{-1}$, and $e=E/c$ and $b=B$ both have units tesla in an oriented orthonormal frame; $\\kappa\\mu_0^{-1}|e|^2$ has units $\\mathrm m^{-2}$. Use the sign convention $K(X,Y)=g(\\nabla_Xn,Y)$ for the future unit normal. Assume\n$$D_ie^i=0,\\qquad D_ib^i=0,\\qquad R(h)+(\\operatorname{tr}_hK)^2-|K|_h^2=2\\Lambda+\\frac{\\kappa}{\\mu_0}(|e|_h^2+|b|_h^2),\\qquad D^jK_{ji}-D_i\\operatorname{tr}_hK=-\\frac{\\kappa}{\\mu_0}(e\\times_h b)_i,$$\nwhere $D$ is the Levi-Civita derivative of $h$. Then there are $T>0$ and a solution of the full source-free Einstein–Maxwell system on $(-T,T)\\times\\mathbb T^3$ with\n$$g\\in L^\\infty H^{s+1}\\cap C^0H^s,\\qquad \\partial_tg\\in L^\\infty H^s\\cap C^0H^{s-1},\\qquad F\\in L^\\infty H^s\\cap C^0H^{s-1}.$$\nThe initial two-form is reconstructed by $F(n,\\mathsf e_i)=-e_i$ and $F(\\mathsf e_i,\\mathsf e_j)=\\epsilon_{ijk}b_k$; no independent Maxwell normal derivative is prescribed. The metric remains Lorentzian with spacelike $t$-levels in a fixed compact admissible state neighborhood. Smooth data give a smooth solution on the same base-order interval. For a fixed harmonic background, on any common local interval with the stated compact-state and uniform $H^s$ bounds, the harmonic-reduced first-order state is unique and continuously dependent on its initial state in $C^0H^{s-1}$. In addition, on any smooth oriented three-manifold, smooth constrained data with these same equations have a smooth full development in a neighborhood of each initial point. No endpoint regularity threshold, strong $C^0H^s$ stability, global continuation, or geodesic completeness is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-emg-smooth-local-geometric-uniqueness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice and Dependent Choice. Let $\\Sigma$ be a smooth oriented three-manifold without boundary, with smooth Riemannian metric $h$, smooth symmetric covariant tensor $K$, and smooth spatial vectors $e,b$. Fix $\\kappa>0$, $\\mu_0>0$, $\\Lambda\\in\\mathbb R$, use signature $(-,+,+,+)$ and $K(X,Y)=g(\\nabla_Xn,Y)$, and suppose the four Einstein–Maxwell constraints of [[thm-emg-gauss-codazzi-maxwell-constraints]] hold. In SI convention use length coordinate $x^0=ct$, dimensionless $g,h$, $K$ in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$, and $e=E/c$, $b=B$ in tesla. Reconstruct the complete initial two-form on $\\mathbb RN\\oplus T\\Sigma$ by\n$$F(N,\\mathsf e_i)=-e_i,\\qquad F(\\mathsf e_i,\\mathsf e_j)=\\epsilon_{ijk}b_k,$$\nwhere $N$ is the future unit normal symbol and $(\\mathsf e_i)$ is a positively oriented $h$-orthonormal frame. Then the data have a smooth time-oriented local source-free Einstein–Maxwell development on an open variable-width neighborhood of the entire initial surface. After restriction to a smaller collar, the interior of its intrinsic domain of dependence is a globally hyperbolic neighborhood with $\\Sigma$ Cauchy. Any two developments of this same model and complete data are related near the entire initial surface by a data-fixing, time-orientation-preserving isometry carrying $F$ to $F$. There is no uniform positive time width for noncompact $\\Sigma$ and no global-in-time conclusion.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "def-emg-local-potentials-and-circle-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,N,o)$ be the smooth, Hausdorff, second-countable, boundaryless oriented and time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold of [[def-emg-lorentzian-forms]]. Its smooth real $k$-forms are sections of $\\bigwedge^kT^*M$, written $\\Omega^k(M)$, and $d$ is the exterior derivative of [[lem-emg-lorentz-hodge-calculus]]. A smooth two-form $F$ is **closed** when $dF=0$ and **exact** when $F=dA$ for some smooth real one-form $A$ on the whole base.\n\nBy [[def-de-rham-cohomology]], $H^2_{\\mathrm{dR}}(M)=Z^2(M)/B^2(M)$, where $Z^2(M)$ is the vector space of closed smooth two-forms and $B^2(M)$ is the vector space of exact smooth two-forms. Thus, for closed $F$, the statement $[F]=0$ means exactly that $F=dA$ for a global smooth one-form $A$. This equivalence is the quotient definition; it does not assert that every closed form is exact.\n\nLet $U(1)=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C:|z|=1\\}$ with complex multiplication, the subspace topology and the usual smooth circle structure. A **normalized circle-connection datum** on $M$ consists of a supplied countable open cover $(U_i)_{i\\in I}$, smooth transition maps $g_{ij}:U_i\\cap U_j\\to U(1)$, smooth real one-forms $a_i\\in\\Omega^1(U_i)$, and a fixed constant $\\kappa_0>0$. Require $g_{ii}=1$ and $g_{ij}g_{jk}=g_{ik}$ on every triple intersection. Around each point of $U_i\\cap U_j$, a smooth real phase lift $f_{ij}$ exists with $g_{ij}=e^{if_{ij}}$; require\n\n$$\na_j=a_i+df_{ij}\n$$\n\non every such phase-lift neighborhood. Different lifts differ locally by an integer multiple of $2\\pi$, so their differentials agree and this compatibility condition is independent of the lift. Equivalently, with the globally defined real one-form $\\lambda(g)=-i\\,g^{-1}dg$ on each overlap, the condition is $a_j=a_i+\\lambda(g_{ij})$.\n\nThe circle is a smooth Lie group and in particular a topological group: multiplication is the restriction of continuous complex multiplication and inversion is complex conjugation. A right principal $U(1)$-bundle has a continuous right action and locally trivial equivariant product charts; those charts make the action free and transitive on each fibre, as defined in [[def-principal-g-bundle-and-associated-fiber-bundle]]. The compatible transition functions are the gluing data for such a bundle, in the convention that a point with fibre coordinate $z_i$ in chart $i$ has coordinate $z_j=g_{ij}^{-1}z_i$ in chart $j$. No global real-valued angle on $U(1)$ is assumed.\n\nThe constant $\\kappa_0$ is a chosen normalization with units of webers; it is not Planck's constant and is not a unit of electric charge. On a chart whose coordinates have length units, the normalized connection components $a_i$ have units $\\mathrm m^{-1}$; the corresponding local potential $A_i:=\\kappa_0a_i$ has components $\\mathrm{Wb}\\,\\mathrm m^{-1}$, and its curvature $F_i:=dA_i=\\kappa_0\\,da_i$ has components $\\mathrm{Wb}\\,\\mathrm m^{-2}$. This definition imposes no flux or charge quantization.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "lem-emg-circle-connection-gluing",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a smooth, Hausdorff, second-countable oriented four-manifold, and let $(U_i,g_{ij},a_i;\\kappa_0)$ be a normalized circle-connection datum as in [[def-emg-local-potentials-and-circle-data]]. Use the set-sized-object and quotient convention of [[def-emg-set-size-and-class-schema]]. Then the chart data construct a smooth Hausdorff second-countable right principal $U(1)$-bundle $\\pi:P\\to M$ and a global smooth real connection one-form $\\omega$ on $P$. Its curvature is basic: there is a smooth closed two-form $f\\in\\Omega^2(M)$ with $d\\omega=\\pi^*f$ and $f|_{U_i}=da_i$. The local potentials $A_i=\\kappa_0a_i$ satisfy $dA_i=F$, where $F=\\kappa_0 f$ is global. For two connections on the same fixed bundle with the same transition functions, their local-form differences are restrictions of one global base one-form; adding a global one-form to all local representatives stays in that bundle sector.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "lem-emg-spacelike-slice-normal-collar",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold, let\n$\\Sigma$ be a smooth three-manifold, and let $i:\\Sigma\\to M$ be a smooth\nspacelike embedding. Suppose a smooth future unit normal field $n$ along\n$i(\\Sigma)$ is supplied. For every $p\\in\\Sigma$ there are an open\nneighborhood $U_p\\subseteq\\Sigma$, a number $\\epsilon_p>0$, and an open\n$V_p\\subseteq M$ such that the restricted map\n$\\Phi_p:(-\\epsilon_p,\\epsilon_p)\\times U_p\\to V_p$ given by\n\n$$\\Phi_p(t,x)=\\gamma_{i(x),n_{i(x)}}(t)$$\n\nis a diffeomorphism, where $\\gamma_{q,v}$ is the maximal affinely\nparameterized metric geodesic with initial data $(q,v)$. At its center,\n\n$$d(\\Phi_p)_{(0,p)}(a\\partial_t+X)=a n_{i(p)}+d i_p(X).$$\n\nThe neighborhoods and time widths may depend on $p$; no globally injective\nnormal exponential map or uniform width is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "lem-emg-closed-two-form-exact-on-cauchy-product",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ and Dependent Choice. Let $(M,g,N)$ be a smooth\ntime-oriented globally hyperbolic Lorentzian four-manifold, and let\n$i:\\Sigma\\hookrightarrow M$ be a smooth spacelike Cauchy embedding. If\n$F,\\bar F\\in\\Omega^2(M)$ are smooth closed two-forms with\n$i^*F=i^*\\bar F$, then $F-\\bar F$ is exact: there is a smooth\n$\\alpha\\in\\Omega^1(M)$ such that $d\\alpha=F-\\bar F$.\n\nHere a closed form has zero exterior derivative, and exactness means being the\nexterior derivative of a globally defined smooth form, as in\n[[def-de-rham-cohomology]].",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F12",
          "source": "thm-gr-smooth-cauchy-temporal-splitting",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axioms of Countable Choice and Dependent Choice\n([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-dependent-choice]]). Let $(M,g,N)$ be a\nglobally hyperbolic time-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold\n([[def-gr-causal-conditions-and-global-hyperbolicity]] clause (c)), and let\n$t$ be the volume time function of [[thm-gr-volume-time-function]]. Then\nthere exist a smooth function $T:M\\to\\mathbb R$ and, with $\\nabla T$ the\ngradient of [[def-gr-inverse-metric-and-musical-isomorphisms]], the\nfollowing hold.\n\n**(a)** $T$ is a temporal function in the sense of\n[[def-gr-causal-conditions-and-global-hyperbolicity]] clause (e): smooth,\n$g(\\nabla T,\\nabla T)<0$ and $g(\\nabla T,N)>0$ everywhere.\n\n**(b)** Every level set $S_c:=T^{-1}(c)$, $c\\in\\mathbb R$, is a Cauchy\nhypersurface ([[def-gr-spacelike-hypersurfaces-and-cauchy-sets]] clauses (a)\nand (c)): it is a spacelike embedded hypersurface and every inextendible\ncausal curve meets it in exactly one parameter value. In particular $T$ is\nsurjective.\n\n**(c)** Along every inextendible future-directed causal curve\n$T\\to+\\infty$, and along every inextendible past-directed causal curve\n$T\\to-\\infty$.\n\n**(d)** The vector field $X:=\\nabla T/g(\\nabla T,\\nabla T)$ is smooth,\nfuture-directed timelike, satisfies $X(T)=1$, and is complete\n([[def-complete-vector-field]]): every maximal integral curve of $X$ is\ndefined on all of $\\mathbb R$, so by\n[[prop-a-vector-field-is-complete-if-and-only-if-its-flow-is-global]] its\nflow $\\Phi:\\mathbb R\\times M\\to M$ is a global smooth flow\n([[def-local-and-global-flow]]).\n\n**(e)** The map $\\Psi:\\mathbb R\\times S_0\\to M$, $\\Psi(s,z):=\\Phi(s,z)$, is\na diffeomorphism and $T(\\Psi(s,z))=s$ for all $(s,z)$; equivalently $M$ is\nfoliated by the Cauchy hypersurfaces $S_c$, each flow line meeting each level\nexactly once.\n\n**(f)** Put $\\beta:=-1/g(\\nabla T,\\nabla T)$, a smooth positive function, and\n$h_T:=g+\\beta\\,dT\\otimes dT$. Then $g=-\\beta\\,dT\\otimes dT+h_T$;\n$h_T(X,\\cdot)=0$; and $h_T$ restricted to $\\ker dT$ is a smooth Riemannian\nmetric, so the decomposition $T_pM=\\mathbb RX_p\\oplus\\ker dT_p$ is\n$g$-orthogonal with $g(X,X)=-\\beta$. Consequently the pullback of $g$ by the\ndiffeomorphism $\\Psi$ has no mixed terms,\n$$\\Psi^*g=-(\\beta\\circ\\Psi)\\,ds^2+h,\\qquad h_{(s,z)}(v,w):=g_{\\Phi(s,z)}\\bigl((d\\Phi_s)_zv,(d\\Phi_s)_zw\\bigr),$$\na sum of a negative definite one-dimensional form and the smooth family of\nRiemannian metrics induced on the levels.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F13",
          "source": "thm-smooth-inverse-function-theorem-on-manifolds",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $F:M\\to N$ be a smooth map and let $p\\in M$. If $dF_p:T_pM\\to T_{F(p)}N$ is\nan isomorphism, then there are open neighbourhoods $U$ of $p$ and $V$ of\n$F(p)$ such that $F|_U:U\\to V$ is a diffeomorphism.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F14",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F14",
          "source": "def-dependent-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a set and let $R \\subseteq X \\times X$ be a binary relation on $X$.\nCall $R$ **entire on $X$** when\n\n$$\\text{for every } x \\in X \\text{ there is } y \\in X \\text{ with } x \\mathbin{R} y .$$\n\nThe **Axiom of Dependent Choice**, written $\\mathrm{DC}$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every nonempty set $X$, every relation $R$ entire on $X$, and every\n> $a \\in X$, there is a function $x : \\mathbb{N} \\to X$ ([[def-function]],\n> [[def-natural-numbers]]) with\n> $$x_0 = a \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad x_n \\mathbin{R} x_{n+1} \\ \\text{ for every } n \\in \\mathbb{N}.$$\n\nHere a sequence in $X$ means a function from $\\mathbb{N}$ to $X$, not necessarily\na real-valued sequence. As everywhere in this library $\\mathbb{N}$ contains $0$, and the sequence is\nindexed from $0$; the term $x_0$ is the prescribed starting point $a$ and every\nlater term is related to its predecessor.\n\n**What DC adds to what came before.** [[def-choice-function]] and\n[[def-axiom-of-choice]] select one element from each member of a family that is\nfixed in advance, and [[def-countable-choice]] does the same for a family indexed\nby $\\mathbb{N}$. In both, the family is given before any selection is made. DC is\nthe principle needed when the $n$-th set to select from is not known until the\nfirst $n$ selections have been made: here the admissible values of $x_{n+1}$ are\nexactly the $R$-successors of $x_n$, so the family being chosen from is built\nalong the choosing. Thus the displayed recursion is directly an instance of\nDC; it is not itself an application of the fixed-family formulation of\ncountable choice. This observation concerns the input to the two axioms and\nmakes no claim about a nonimplication between them.\n\n**The starting point may be dropped.** The formally weaker statement obtained by\ndeleting the clause $x_0 = a$ — for every nonempty $X$ and every entire $R$ there\nis a sequence with $x_n \\mathbin{R} x_{n+1}$ for all $n$ — is an immediate\nconsequence of the form above, since $X$ is nonempty and any of its elements may\nbe taken as $a$. Conversely, assume the start-free form, and fix $X$, $R$ and $a$ as above. Let $P$ be the set of all functions $p:k\\to X$ with nonzero $k\\in\\mathbb N$, $p(0)=a$, and $p(i)\\mathbin R p(i+1)$ whenever $i+1<k$. It is a set by Separation inside $\\mathcal P(\\mathbb N\\times X)$ ([[def-power-set]], [[def-cartesian-product]], [[def-axiom-schema-of-separation]]); a natural is the set of its predecessors ([[lem-nat-order-is-membership]]). The singleton path $\\{(0,a)\\}$ lies in $P$.\n\nOn $P$ let $p\\mathbin S q$ mean that $q$ extends $p$ by exactly one new last entry. This relation is entire: for one particular $p:k\\to X$, write $k=j+1$ ([[lem-nat-nonzero-is-successor]]); an $R$-successor $y$ of $p(j)$ exists, and $q=p\\cup\\{(k,y)\\}$ is the required extension. This is existential instantiation for that path, not a simultaneous choice of successors. Start-free DC now supplies a sequence $(p_n)$ with $p_n\\mathbin S p_{n+1}$; its first path need not be the singleton path, but every path in $P$ already starts at $a$.\n\nTheir graphs are nested. By induction ([[thm-induction-principle]]), $p_n$ has at least $n+1$ entries: the initial path is nonempty, and every step adds one entry. Hence $x=\\bigcup_{n\\in\\mathbb N}p_n$ is a function with domain $\\mathbb N$ and values in $X$. Indeed, nestedness makes all common values agree, every natural index eventually lies in a path, and no path has indices outside $\\mathbb N$. Each path starts at $a$, so $x(0)=a$; for any $i$, a sufficiently long path contains both $i$ and $i+1$, giving $x(i)\\mathbin R x(i+1)$. Replacement and Union form this union of the sequence of graphs. Thus the start-free and prescribed-start forms are equivalent in ZF. Every use below retains the prescribed-start convention.\n\n**$R$ need not be an order and the terms need not be distinct.** What DC delivers\nis a sequence, that is a function $\\mathbb{N} \\to X$, not a chain in the\norder-theoretic sense ([[def-chain]]). The relation may be symmetric, and the\nsequence may repeat a value or be constant; all that is asserted is\n$x_n \\mathbin{R} x_{n+1}$ at every index.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The four constraints in [F5] are exactly the initial constraints for [F4]\nwith the same $K$, orientation, stress normalization, and definitions of\n$e,b$. The Cauchy data prescribe the magnetic two-form\n$\\beta_b(X,Y)=\\operatorname{vol}_h(b,X,Y)$ and the electric covector determined\nby $e$. In any resulting future-normal orthonormal frame, [F4] records their\ncomponents as $F(n,\\mathsf e_i)=-e_i$ and\n$F(\\mathsf e_i,\\mathsf e_j)=\\epsilon_{ijk}b_k$. These are the six independent\ncomponents of the initial field strength and require no global potential.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Apply [F6] to these smooth constrained data and choice assumptions [A3].\nIts arbitrary-slice clause gives a source-free solution locally near each\ninitial point. The stronger geometric patching and uniqueness supplier [F7]\nuses the same complete data and exact field-strength conventions; it yields a\nsingle smooth development on a variable-width neighborhood of the entire\ninitial surface and a smaller globally hyperbolic Cauchy neighborhood. No\npositive uniform width is inferred. The resulting spacelike Cauchy embedding\nhas a future unit normal by the definition of spacelike hypersurface; [F10]\nthen supplies normal geodesic charts at each point, again with no uniform\nwidth. These charts justify the local observer-frame reconstruction after\nthe spacetime exists; they are not used to assume local existence.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "A3",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F10",
            "F14"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Pull the initial local transition data in [A2] back to\n$\\mathbb R\\times\\Sigma$ by the projection to $\\Sigma$. Their cocycle and\npotential identities persist, so [F8]–[F9] construct a reference connection\nin the same normalized bundle sector, with curvature $\\bar F$ satisfying\n$i^*\\bar F=\\beta_b=i^*F$. Both $F$ and $\\bar F$ are closed by [F4] and\n[F9]. On the globally hyperbolic Cauchy neighborhood supplied in step 2.1,\napply [F11] to obtain a global one-form $\\alpha$ with $F-\\bar F=d\\alpha$. Adding\n$\\alpha/\\kappa_0$ to the normalized local connection representatives\npreserves their transition functions by [F9] and changes the physical\ncurvature by $\\kappa_0d(\\alpha/\\kappa_0)=d\\alpha$, hence to exactly $F$. Thus the\nfield-strength solution lies in the supplied fixed circle-bundle sector;\nthe temporal splitting and inverse-function inputs are exactly those used in\n[F10]–[F11].",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "A3",
            "step 2.1",
            "F4",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "F11",
            "F12",
            "F13",
            "F10"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-2",
          "claim": "For any other solution of the same model with the same complete data,\n[F2] gives the same source-free field-strength system. [F7] then supplies a\ndata-fixing, time-orientation-preserving isometry on a neighborhood of the\ninitial surface carrying $g$ and $F$ to the other development. The argument\nasserts uniqueness of these field-strength fields, not uniqueness of a local\nconnection representative.",
          "step": "3.2",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "By [A1], the selected matter variations vanish and $j=T^m=0$. The\nsolution from step 2.1 satisfies the field equations in [F4], and step 3.1\nrealizes its $F$ as the curvature of a connection in the fixed sector. The\nconverse direction of [F2] therefore makes this configuration stationary for\nthe adopted action under every allowed compact variation. This is a\nconditional deduction from the action branch, not a proof that the model\ndescribes nature.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "Steps 2.1, 3.1, 4.1 and 3.2 prove the stated local existence, bundle\nsector, action stationarity and geometric uniqueness. No empirical premise,\nglobal-time claim, or maximal-development assertion has entered.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "step 3.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
